# The Semgrep job's tool.
#
# pip-compile turns this into semgrep.txt: every package, exact versions AND hashes, installed with
# `--require-hashes`. Dependabot keeps it current by re-running pip-compile on the Python its
# header names (3.11), so a bump never pins a set that cannot be installed together. Edit
# THIS file, then regenerate (run in python:3.11-slim):
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --strip-extras --output-file=semgrep.txt semgrep.in
#
# semgrep.txt is the whole closure, PyJWT included, and the job installs it alone with pip's
# dependency check on. semgrep 1.178.0 pinned pyjwt~=2.13.0, every release of which is vulnerable,
# so PyJWT was compiled out (--unsafe-package) and pinned in a second file installed with
# --no-deps. semgrep 1.179.0 requires pyjwt>=2.15.0,<3, which is safe, so that split is gone.
semgrep
